 RePaint-NeRF is a framework for editing 3D content in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) using text prompts by leveraging a pre-trained diffusion model to guide changes in designated 3D content, ensuring view consistency and scene integrity. It solves the problem of modifying specific objects within a complex 3D scene without altering the surrounding environment or introducing artifacts like "black holes" where previously occluded geometry is exposed. It sets itself apart from standard NeRF optimization by decoupling the process into a semantic mask extraction stage and a diffusion-guided editing stage, using CLIP features for segmentation and Stable Diffusion for generation.
 
-### Supersampling Baseline
-A baseline method for evaluating NeRF on multiscale data by training on full-resolution images and using brute-force supersampling during evaluation, or training on multiscale data with supersampling during both training and evaluation [S21]. For every pixel, it casts multiple jittered rays sampled uniformly at random through the spatial footprint of the pixel, renders each ray with NeRF, and uses the mean of those rendered values as the predicted color in the loss function [S21]. The full-resolution supersampling baseline relies on 'oracle' scale information that does not exist for most datasets [S21]. The 16x supersampling baseline performs worse than mip-NeRF and is significantly more expensive during both training and evaluation [S21].
-
-### Two-stage training for proposers
-A training schedule split into two equal-duration phases to stabilize learning of a trainable proposer network in NeRF-ID [S22, unofficial]. Phase 1 trains the learnt proposer to mimic the heuristic proposer by minimizing the L2 matching loss between their proposal sets [S22, unofficial]. Phase 2 trains all network components end-to-end, resetting the optimizer state and performing a linear warmup for 1,000 SGD steps to reduce shock from switching regimes [S22, unofficial]. Training from scratch sometimes works but often fails; two-stage training is consistently effective [S22, unofficial].
-
-### Surface-aware density formulation
-A method to extract high-quality surfaces from NeRF by modeling geometry as a distance function or occupancy field and converting its output to density using a probability density function [S23]. It incorporates an eikonal term to encourage geometric consistency, enforcing that the gradient norm of the learned SDF is equal to one [S23]. The density distribution is modeled as $\Phi_S(x) = \frac{se^{-sx}}{1+e^{-sx}}$, where $s$ is a scaling parameter and $x$ is the SDF or occupancy value [S23].
-
-### JaxNeRF
-A re-implementation of the original NeRF in JAX, used as a benchmark comparison for early works seeking to improve on training and rendering speed [S24]. It is slightly faster and more suited for distributed computing than the original TensorFlow implementation of NeRF [S24].
-
-### Gradient Detachment in Training
-A training strategy where the object field component is detached from the backbone to prevent gradient backpropagation to the rendering part, ensuring independent optimization [S25, unofficial]. With detach, the object field component output is detached so gradients do not backpropagate to the NeRF backbone; without detach, gradients from the object field component backpropagate to the NeRF backbone, causing mutual influence [S25, unofficial]. The hyper-parameter $\Delta d$ for empty 3D point losses ($\ell_{3d \text{ empty}} + \ell_{3d \text{ obj}}$) is tested at 0.025, 0.05, 0.10 meters, with 0.05 meters found optimal [S25, unofficial]. With detach achieves better scene rendering and decomposition quality, while without detach causes a significant drop in rendering quality [S25, unofficial].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ per iteration, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (number of rays), and $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S11, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus $O(\Theta)$ for model parameters [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. VRAM usage can be mitigated by chunking ray batches [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In the original NeRF implementation, training on Blender synthetic data (800x800) takes approximately 5 hours on a single GPU for 500,000 iterations [S5, unofficial]. Training on 128x128 images for 250 epochs converges significantly faster [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training images and scene complexity. Larger batch sizes ($2^{10}$ vs $2^{14}$) trade off data loading performance for GPU occupancy [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, robust convergence on multi-view synthetic data, high-quality novel view synthesis [S5, S11, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence (requires hundreds of thousands of iterations), prone to overfitting on sparse views, struggles with dynamic scenes and transient objects without extensions [S1][S11, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required for static scenes with multi-view input and computational time is not a primary constraint [S5, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications, dynamic scenes, or sparse-view scenarios without additional regularization or architectural modifications [S1][S11, unofficial].
